# Shemá — forms, submissions and the leader link (BE-12 · OBT-401)
#
# The loop this file walks is the product's own, and it has five steps: a coordinator mints a
# link, sends it, the leader answers, a coordinator imports, the system archives. Two of the
# five happen with no `Authorization` header at all — they are the only routes in this module
# that do, and they are the reason to run this file by hand rather than trust the tests.
#
# Four things are worth watching while you run it:
#
#   1. the raw token appears in exactly one response, the one that mints the link. The listing
#      does not carry it and the row stores only its SHA-256. Copy it when you see it.
#   2. `GET /api/shema/intake/{token}` answers the form and the language name and nothing else
#      of the project. Put a location, a base and a prayer request on the record first and look
#      for them in the answer — a forwarded WhatsApp message reaches whoever it reaches.
#   3. the answer comes back `202`, and the record does not move. That is the design: the link
#      deposits and a coordinator applies, because `save_project` wants an actor the audit
#      trail can name and a version somebody read, and a link has neither.
#   4. sending the same answer twice changes nothing — one archive, one notice, one progress
#      entry. A leader who forwards the same file twice must not double a chapter count.
#
# Before running: seed the app and its roles, then grant one and a region.
#   uv run python scripts/seed_apps_roles.py
#   uv run python scripts/grant_app_role.py coordenacao.local@test.com shema coordinator
#   # the region scope is this module's own table; set it through the service or by SQL:
#   #   INSERT INTO shema_user_regions (user_id, region_key) VALUES ('<id>', 'south-america');
#
# `shema_record.http` beside this one creates the record these routes need.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000
@slug = guarani-mbya

### Log in — the shared /api/auth, as every product here does
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "coordenacao.local@test.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

###
@token = {{login.response.body.access_token}}

### Mint a link — project-scoped, expiring, and the only sight of the raw token
# `expiresAt` is optional: without it the link lives 45 days, which is longer than one Pulse
# cycle and shorter than two. Ask for more than 90 days and it is refused.
# @name minted
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake-links
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "projectId": "{{slug}}"
}

###
@link = {{minted.response.body.token}}
@linkId = {{minted.response.body.id}}

### The links out on this project — note that none of them carries a token
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake-links?project_id={{slug}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The form the leader opens — NO Authorization header, deliberately
# The whole of what the link grants. `kind`, `definitionVersion`, `languageName`, `expiresAt`,
# `fields`, `locationWithheld` — and nothing else. If you can find the project's location, its
# base, a contact or a count in this answer, that is the bug this route exists not to have.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake/{{link}}

### The leader answers — NO Authorization header either. Answers 202 and writes no record.
# `definitionVersion` has to be the one the link was minted with; the server checks it against
# the link rather than using it to pick a form. A field this version does not have is refused,
# and so is the whole submission with it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake/{{link}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "definitionVersion": 1,
  "answers": {
    "submittedBy": "Kuaray",
    "period": "2026-09",
    "voice": "A equipe gravou Marcos 1 a 9 com os anciãos da aldeia.",
    "blockers": "O gerador queimou; sem energia não há gravação.",
    "bookProgress": [
      {
        "id": "mrk",
        "name": "Marcos",
        "chapters": 16,
        "translated": 9,
        "communityChecked": 4,
        "mentorApproved": 0
      }
    ],
    "prayerRequest": "Orem pela travessia do rio na cheia.",
    "prayerVisibility": "coordenacao"
  }
}

### The same answer again — 202, and nothing changes. Run it and look at the inbox after.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake/{{link}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "definitionVersion": 1,
  "answers": {
    "submittedBy": "Kuaray",
    "period": "2026-09",
    "voice": "A equipe gravou Marcos 1 a 9 com os anciãos da aldeia.",
    "blockers": "O gerador queimou; sem energia não há gravação.",
    "bookProgress": [
      {
        "id": "mrk",
        "name": "Marcos",
        "chapters": 16,
        "translated": 9,
        "communityChecked": 4,
        "mentorApproved": 0
      }
    ],
    "prayerRequest": "Orem pela travessia do rio na cheia.",
    "prayerVisibility": "coordenacao"
  }
}

### A submission that does not match — every fault named at once, and nothing archived
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake/{{link}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "definitionVersion": 1,
  "answers": {
    "period": "septembro",
    "prayerVisibility": "todo-mundo",
    "rainfall": "forte"
  }
}

### The inbox — scoped to the regions this coordinator reaches
# @name inbox
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/forms/submissions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

###
@submissionId = {{inbox.response.body.0.id}}

### One submission opened — the answers as they arrived, beside the form they answered
# This is where the voice and the blockers are read. They map to no record column on purpose:
# a Pulse that replaced a coordinator's status comments with a leader's free text would be the
# record being edited through the weakest credential in the system.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/forms/submissions/{{submissionId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Read the record to get the version the import will quote
# @name record
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/projects/{{slug}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### Import it — the coordinator's step, guarded exactly as a typed save is
# The progress goes through `save_project`, so the history entry carries `fromField` and
# `formType` and is otherwise indistinguishable from one a coordinator typed. Run it twice:
# the second call answers 200, applies nothing and does not double the chapter count.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/forms/submissions/{{submissionId}}/import
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
If-Match: {{record.response.headers.ETag}}
X-Shema-Local-Date: 2026-09-11

### File an answer that arrived some other way — paper, a voice note, a bad phone line
# Same validation, same archive, same idempotency, and an actor the trail can name.
# `definitionVersion` is optional here and means *the version standing today*.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/forms/submissions
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json
If-Match: "2"
X-Shema-Local-Date: 2026-09-11

{
  "projectId": "{{slug}}",
  "answers": {
    "submittedBy": "Jaxuka",
    "period": "2026-10",
    "voice": "Lido por telefone; a ligação caiu duas vezes."
  }
}

### Revoke the link — and then try the two intake routes again
# Revocation beats expiry: a link taken back stays taken back after its clock runs out. Both
# intake routes answer 404 afterwards, with a message that says which of the three it is.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake-links/{{linkId}}/revoke
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### The form behind a revoked link — 404, and the message says why
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake/{{link}}

### The rate limit — run this one in a loop from a shell rather than by clicking
# Two buckets are stacked on each intake route: one keyed on the caller's address and one on
# the link's own token. The per-link bucket is the tighter of the two on purpose — a link
# forwarded to a hundred phones is one token, and an address bucket never notices it.
#
#   for i in $(seq 1 25); do
#     curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' {{baseUrl}}/api/shema/intake/{{link}}
#   done
#
# The last few answer 429.
